#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "506f52f3-8093-5bee-a2f5-f30f064dc715" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/id #uuid "caa282bb-e637-522c-a2da-b384b1ff855a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10012" :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e9a879522107eb2b2381a688d1173fdf6a93a8b81686fd2f21c815008ec35df.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10$. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=3$." :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3)$, and\n\n$$\nf'(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\non this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,3$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-2) &= 3 \\\\\nf(3) &= f(-2) + \\int_{-2}^{3}f^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= 3 + (-10) \\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{3,-7\\} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}}=3\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max}=-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100698 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "be6b45d4-9257-5a84-8e19-50a315871d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-442" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $1 \\leq x \\leq 5$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below. Find a value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2$.\n\n![](images/e4/e4327bc76ae23907fb628cc9e9d5d045ab773070f9ce8fc2972dcf78e434b24d.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5)$, and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=2$ and $x=4$. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,4,5$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7f129ef5fe5d219f1bf57425682cea13ff657b9a539dc7ae0b2d172bf37c2c28.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= -2 \\\\\nf(2) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{2}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 + \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nf(4) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{4}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 + \\frac{3}{2} - 1 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\nf(5) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{5}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 + \\frac{3}{2} - 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\left\\lbrace -2,-\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{3}{2},-1 \\right\\rbrace = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max}=2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100699 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/id #uuid "9fbd4cac-e923-57fd-94f3-79e323195d1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-443" :question/problem "The function $f$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,6]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,6]$ given that $f(0)=1$.\n\n![](images/7c/7ca8e07d3438890b4f1e220e491d4a20f35c7473a31e76426b9a1fa13ed6926c.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6)$, and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=4$. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,4,6$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0) &= 1 \\\\\nf(4) &= f(0) + \\int_{0}^{4}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 1 + 6 \\\\\n&= 7 \\\\\nf(6) &= f(0) + \\int_{0}^{6}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 1 + 6 - 2 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{1, 7, 5\\} = 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}}=7\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max}=4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "506f52f3-8093-5bee-a2f5-f30f064dc715" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdd0cee-d5dc-55dd-b18b-edd5c5d143b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098296} {:answer/id #uuid "a95de19e-8a0c-5ea7-a40e-8653db2879e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098297} {:answer/id #uuid "c73cb9d0-0bfe-5fae-8bc1-10f38468113d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098298} {:answer/id #uuid "db6c4cce-5a1e-5047-98c5-fd057127d3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098299} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8cc2f3-9815-5b94-8ea4-2375914bf19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098300}] :answer-field/id #uuid "aaaebb3c-11a8-5d61-b73a-97d31a924d49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098295}] :question/id #uuid "8b935d32-f672-54cd-918e-5cf82b270c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141616" :question/problem "![](images/06/0669cf5e31d559c70c22c5154c0e43638eb5df8a11bedab1e5eea4dc4d7ab90f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 2, 2]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $7$. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 2, 2]$ given that $f(-2) = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d